    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d849">

                <pb facs="0751" n="739" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg739"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867.24/450: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kelley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-10-22T18:00:00+03:00">October 22, 1942—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1052. Department’s 461, September 23, paragraph 1. I have ascertained that
                    Turkish-Swedish negotiations for charter of three Swedish vessels by Turkey have
                    broken down because Germans have refused to allow these ships to leave Baltic.
                    It is opinion of Foreign Office that there is no possibility of negotiations
                    being renewed.</p>
                <p>Department will recall that in chrome negotiations the Minister for Foreign
                        Affairs<anchor n="65" xml:id="d849-f1"/> requested British to let him have
                    5,000 tons of chrome for delivery to Sweden in connection with charter of
                    Swedish vessels. It appears that Swedes insisted upon delivery of chrome as soon
                    as first Swedish vessel arrived in Turk waters. Turks now admit their belief
                    that chrome demand had been made at German instigation and that chrome would
                    probably have gone to Germany.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kelley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
